" (https://real.mtak.hu/236600/1/13537-PDFfile-58658-1-10-20260115.pdf)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://real.mtak.hu/236600/1/13537-PDFfile-58658-1-10-20260115.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1532\nL0@P0: Cyclic Ordering of Split Matroids\nL1@P0: Krist´of B´erczia Aron J´anosik ´ b Bence M´atrav¨olgyic\nL2@P0: Submitted: Nov 1, 2024; Accepted: Dec 30, 2025; Published: Jan 23, 2026\nL3@P0: ©The authors. Released under the CC BY-ND license (International 4.0).\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: There is a long list of open questions rooted in the same underlying problem: un\u0002derstanding the structure of bases or common bases of matroids. These conjectures\nL6@P0: suggest that matroids may possess much stronger structural properties than are\nL7@P0: currently known. One example is related to cyclic orderings of matroids. A rank-r\nL8@P0: matroid is called cyclically orderable if its ground set admits a cyclic ordering such\nL9@P0: that any interval of r consecutive elements forms a basis. In this paper, we show\nL10@P0: that if the ground set of a split matroid decomposes into pairwise disjoint bases,\nL11@P0: then it is cyclically orderable. This result answers a conjecture of Kajitani, Ueno,\nL12@P0: and Miyano in a special case, and also strengthens Gabow’s conjecture for this class\nL13@P0: of matroids. Our proof is algorithmic, hence it provides a procedure for determin\u0002ing a cyclic ordering in question using a polynomial number of independence oracle\nL14@P0: calls.\nL15@P0: Mathematics Subject Classifications: 05B35\nL16@P0: 1 Introduction\nL17@P0: Throughout the paper, we denote a matroid by M = (S, B), where S is a finite ground set\nL18@P0: and B is the family of bases, satisfying the so-called basis axioms: (B1) ∅ ∈ B, and (B2) for\nL19@P0: any B1, B2 ∈ B and e ∈ B1 − B2, there exists f ∈ B2 − B1 such that B1 − e + f ∈ B. The\nL20@P0: latter property, called the basis exchange axiom, is one of the most fundamental tools in\nL21@P0: matroid theory. Nevertheless, it only provides a local characterization of the relationship\nL22@P0: between bases, which presents a significant stumbling block to further progress.\nL23@P0: A rank-r matroid M = (S, B) with |S| = n is cyclically orderable if there exists an\nL24@P0: ordering S = {s1, . . . , sn} such that {si, si+1, . . . , si+r−1} ∈ B for all i ∈ [n], where indices\nL25@P0: are understood in a cyclic order. While studying the structure of symmetric exchanges in\nL26@P0: aMTA-ELTE Matroid Optimization Research Group and HUN-REN-ELTE Egerv´ary Research Group,\nL27@P0: Department of Operations Research, E¨otv¨os Lor´and University, and HUN-REN Alfr´ed R´enyi\nL28@P0: Institute of Mathematics, Budapest, Hungary (kristof.berczi@ttk.elte.hu).\nL29@P0: bMTA-ELTE Matroid Optimization Research Group, Department of Operations Research, E¨otv¨os\nL30@P0: Lor´and University, Budapest, Hungary (aron@janosik.hu).\nL31@P0: c\nL32@P0: IDSIA, USI-SUPSI, Switzerland (bence.matravoelgyi@idsia.ch).\nL33@P0-1: the electronic journal of combinatorics 33(1) (2026), #P1.20 https://doi.org/10.37236/13537matroids, Gabow [12] formulated a beautiful conjecture, stating that every matroid whose\nL34@P1: ground set decomposes into two disjoint bases is cyclically orderable. This question was\nL35@P1: also raised independently by Wiedemann [20] and by Cordovil and Moreira [6]. The\nL36@P1: conjecture makes a stronger claim: for a fixed partition, the cyclic ordering can be chosen\nL37@P1: such that the elements of the two bases in the partition form contiguous intervals.\nL38@P1: Conjecture 1 (Gabow). Let M = (S, B) be a matroid and S = B1 ∪ B2 be a partition\nL39@P1: of the ground set into two disjoint bases. Then, M has a cyclic ordering in which the\nL40@P1: elements of B1 and B2 form intervals.\nL41@P1: It is not difficult to see that the statement holds for strongly base orderable matroids.\nL42@P1: The conjecture was settled for graphic matroids [14, 6, 20], sparse paving matroids [5],\nL43@P1: matroids of rank at most 4 [16] and 5 [15], split matroids [4], and regular matroids [2].\nL44@P1: However, the existence of a cyclic ordering remains open in general, even without the\nL45@P1: constraint of the bases forming intervals.\nL46@P1: In [14], Kajitani, Ueno, and Miyano proposed a conjecture that would provide a full\nL47@P1: characterization of cyclically orderable matroids. A matroid M = (S, B) with rank func\u0002tion rM is called uniformly dense if |S| · rM(X) > rM(S) · |X| holds for all X ⊆ S. It is\nL48@P1: not difficult to see that a cyclically orderable matroid is necessarily uniformly dense as\nL49@P1: well, and the conjecture states that this condition is also sufficient.\nL50@P1: Conjecture 2 (Kajitani, Ueno, and Miyano). A matroid is cyclically orderable if and\nL51@P1: only if it is uniformly dense.\nL52@P1: Despite the fact that the conjecture would provide entirely new insights into the\nL53@P1: structure of matroids, very little progress has been made so far. Van den Heuvel and\nL54@P1: Thomass´e [18] showed that the conjecture is true if |S| and r(S) are coprimes, and Bonin’s\nL55@P1: result [5] for sparse paving matroids remains true also in this more general setting.\nL56@P1: It is worth taking a moment to consider the interpretation of the uniformly dense\nL57@P1: property. By the matroid union theorem of Edmonds and Fulkerson [7], the ground set\nL58@P1: of a matroid M = (S, B) can be covered by k bases if and only if k · rM(X) > |X| holds\nL59@P1: for all X ⊆ S. Using this, a matroid is uniformly dense if and only if its ground set can\nL60@P1: be covered by d|S|/rM(S)e bases. In other words, the ground set can be decomposed in\nL61@P1: ‘almost’ disjoint bases, where almost means that the total overlapping between distinct\nL62@P1: bases is bounded by rM(S) − 1. In particular, any matroid whose ground set decomposes\nL63@P1: into pairwise disjoint bases is uniformly dense. This observation motivates the following\nL64@P1: strengthening of Gabow’s conjecture.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://agostoni.web.elte.hu/bboard/egyeni-kutatomunka/EK-2024tavasz/Janosik-Aron-2024tavasz-Beszamolo.pdf)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://agostoni.web.elte.hu/bboard/egyeni-kutatomunka/EK-2024tavasz/Janosik-Aron-2024tavasz-Beszamolo.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0@P0: On some conjectures about basis exchanges and\nL1@P0: orderings\nL2@P0: J´anosik Aron ´\nL3@P0: Advisor: B´erczi Krist´of\nL4@P0: Continuing the research from last semester, I started with investigating the\nL5@P0: conjecture of Kajitani et. al. [5] about cyclically orderable matroids.\nL6@P0: Definition 1. A matroid M of rank r is cyclically orderable if there exists\nL7@P0: a cyclic permutation of the elements of M such that any r consecutive elements\nL8@P0: form a base.\nL9@P0: Definition 2. A matroid M with ground set S is called uniformly dense if\nL10@P0: r(S) · |X| ≤ r(X) · |S| holds for every X ⊆ S.\nL11@P0: Conjecture 1 (Kajitani, Ueno, Miyano). A matroid M is cyclically orderable if\nL12@P0: and only if it is uniformly dense.\nL13@P0: Van den Heuvel and Thomass´e [6] showed that the conjecture is true if |S|\nL14@P0: and r(S) are coprimes.\nL15@P0: Theorem 3 (van den Heuvel, Thomass´e). Let M be a loopless matroid such\nL16@P0: that |S| and r(S) are coprime. There is a cyclic ordering of S such that every\nL17@P0: r(S) cyclically consecutive elements are bases of M if and only if M is uniformly\nL18@P0: dense.\nL19@P0: Throughout the proof, they use a technical theorem about weighted matroids.\nL20@P0: A weighted matroid is a matroid with a weight function w : S → R on the ground\nL21@P0: set.\nL22@P0: Theorem 4 (van den Heuvel, Thomass´e). Let (M, w) be a loopless weighted\nL23@P0: matroid with non-negative integer weights and D a positive integer. Then there\nL24@P0-1: 1exists a mapping φ : S → {1, 2, . . . , D} such that for every x ∈ {1, 2, . . . , D}, the\nL25@P1: set {s ∈ S | x ∈ [φ(s), φ(s) + w(s))} is independent if and only if for all X ⊆ S,\nL26@P1: we have w(X) ≤ D · r(X).\nL27@P1: Proof of Theorem 3. If there exists a cyclic ordering of S, then any element of\nL28@P1: the ground set is contained in r of the bases formed by r consecutive elements.\nL29@P1: Let us denote the aforementioned bases by B1, . . . , Bn, where |S| = n. So for any\nL30@P1: X ⊆ S, r · |X| = |X ∩ B1| + . . . + |X ∩ Bn| ≤ n · r(X).\nL31@P1: For the other direction, we use Theorem 4 with w(s) = r for all s ∈ S and\nL32@P1: D = n. The condition is satisfied by the assumption of uniform density. So there\nL33@P1: exists a mapping φ : S → {1, 2, . . . , n} such that for every x ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n},\nL34@P1: the set Sx = {s ∈ S | x ∈ {φ(s), φ(s) + 1, . . . , φ(s) + r − 1}} is independent.\nL35@P1: So since all weights are r, the elements mapped to the cyclic interval Ir(x) =\nL36@P1: {x, x + 1, . . . , x + r − 1} form an independent set. By double counting, each\nL37@P1: interval needs to have r elements mapped to it, because by independence, there\nL38@P1: cannot be more than r mapped to any of the intervals.\nL39@P1: Suppose that there is an x ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n} without a preimage :((((. Then\nL40@P1: there are r elements mapped to {x + 1, . . . , x + r − 1}, so x + r has no preimage\nL41@P1: either. By repeating this argument, x + 2r, x + 3r, . . . have no preimage, and\nL42@P1: by the assumption of gcd(n, r) = 1, this process reaches all elements, which is\nL43@P1: a contradiction. So every x has a preimage, which means they all have exactly\nL44@P1: one, and that gives a cyclic ordering given that any r consecutive elements form\nL45@P1: an independent set.\nL46@P1: Throughout the proof, we only use the assumption of gcd(n, r) = 1 once, in\nL47@P1: the end. If gcd(n, r) = g, we can repeat the same proof, except that in the last\nL48@P1: paragraph we look at the preimage of intervals of length g. If one of them, say\nL49@P1: {x, x+1, . . . , x+g−1}, has a preimage of size less than g, then {x+g, . . . , x+r−1}\nL50@P1: has a preimage of size more than r −g, so then {x + r, . . . , x + r + g −1} has also\nL51@P1: a preimage of size less than g. By repeating this argument, we get n/g disjoint\nL52@P1: intervals of length g which all have a preimage of size smaller than g, which is a\nL53@P1: contradiction. This means that all intervals of length g have a preimage of size\nL54@P1: g. This reduces the original problem in the following way.\nL55@P1-2: 2Question 5. Let M be a matroid with ground set S, |S| = n and rank r, and\nL56@P2: gcd(n, r) = g. If the ground set is partitioned into n/g groups with size g along\nL57@P2: a circle and any r/g cyclically consecutive groups form a basis, then is there an\nL58@P2: ordering within the groups such that any r consecutive elements form a basis?\nL59@P2: If the answer was yes, then it would solve the original conjecture for any\nL60@P2: matroid, because we always have such a partitioning by the modified proof.\nL61@P2: However, the answer is no, we found multiple counterexamples. At first, we\nL62@P2: did not assume that g is the greatest common divisor, just any common divisor.\nL63@P2: For that, sparse paving matroids give a counterexample with n = 8, r = 4, g = 2.\nL64@P2: For that, we used the hypergraph representation of sparse paving matroids, given\nL65@P2: by B´erczi et. al. [2]. Let the groups be {a1, a2}, {a3, a4}, {a5, a6}, {a7, a8}. Let the\nL66@P2: hyperedges be {a1, a2, a3, a8}, {a1, a2, a4, a7}, {a3, a5, a6, a7}, {a4, a5, a6, a8}. Those\nL67@P2: all have intersection of size at most 2, so they represent a sparse paving matroid.\nL68@P2: Then the ordering of {a1, a2} and {a5, a6} does not matter, but all the 4 ways in\nL69@P2: which {a3, a4} and {a7, a8} could be ordered, are excluded by one of the hyper\u0002edges.\nL70@P2: With the assumption of g = gcd(n, r), there is also a counterexample with\nL71@P2: sparse paving matroids. Let n = 10, r = 4, g = 2. Let the groups be {a1, a2}, {a3, a4},\n"
